Connect a mineral formula with atoms, sites, charge, and measured composition (@mp-atomic: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mineralogy-and-petrology, #source "../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP", #source "../../../../hephaestus/chemistry/general-chemistry/General-chemistry.lingua#r_206GS6CVXD8WSY1VX7PRF7136V", #mineralogy-and-petrology "../../../../hephaestus/chemistry/general-chemistry/states/Crystals-disorder-and-material-properties.lingua#r_2D2PGMBBES807ABNV8V0A46JZA") { r_559FNR7H67KS8GMHM91CX3DQ4F
**Read:** [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the complete Mineral Chemistry chapter and normalization examples; [General chemistry](../../../../hephaestus/chemistry/general-chemistry/General-chemistry.lingua#r_206GS6CVXD8WSY1VX7PRF7136V), amount, bonding, composition, and measurement foundations as needed.

A mineral formula summarizes a structural/chemical account, not merely an alphabetical list of detected elements. Distinguish elemental mass fraction, oxide-equivalent mass fraction, mole proportions, atoms per formula unit, site occupancy, and oxidation state. Their denominators and assumptions differ.

Use the chemistry foundations for moles, bonding, and charge. An oxide report often expresses measured elements as conventional oxides; it does not establish that those separate oxide minerals occur in the sample. Reporting total iron as FeO also does not independently measure every iron atom's oxidation state.

A site model places particular occupants into an identified structural arrangement with multiplicities, vacancies, and constraints. A charge-balanced candidate formula may be chemically possible without being the correct structure, oxidation state, or species. Several site allocations can fit the same bulk elemental information.

**Work:** convert a simple ideal mineral formula to element and oxide-equivalent proportions with stated atomic weights, then reverse the calculation. Explain which information is lost in the conversion. Compare a measured composition with an ideal endmember, preserving analytical total, uncertainty, possible mixed analysis, and unmeasured components.

Read the entire mineral-chemistry chapter. Follow [Connect crystal structure, defects, and composition with material properties](../../../../hephaestus/chemistry/general-chemistry/states/Crystals-disorder-and-material-properties.lingua#r_2D2PGMBBES807ABNV8V0A46JZA) for shared crystalline/disordered-material foundations; its mineralogy continuation develops lattices quantitatively. Later original normalization work will expose both the useful bookkeeping and the limits of inferring a full mineral identity from an oxide table.

**Continue:** [Connect crystal structure, defects, and composition with material properties](../../../../hephaestus/chemistry/general-chemistry/states/Crystals-disorder-and-material-properties.lingua#r_2D2PGMBBES807ABNV8V0A46JZA).

